  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strong surface high around 1035 hPa continues to build in over \nsouthern Quebec, NY and New England tonight.  Near ideal radiational \ncooling conditions set-up with mostly clear to partly cloudy skies \nand light to calm winds, as some cirrus may drift in over the top of \nthe mid and upper level ridge after midnight.  The low-levels are \nvery dry with PWATS close to 0.25\", which are 1-2 STDEVS below \nnormal based on th latest NAEFS guidance.  Lows will fall into the \n20s to around 30F (close to a MAV/MET/NBM blend). The coldest \nreadings may be over the southern Dacks and the sheltered \nvalleys north of Albany. The thin cirrus should diminish \ntomorrow morning with the sfc anticyclone centered over NY and \nNew England with mostly sunny skies by the afternoon and light \nwinds. Max temps will be seasonable with 50s to around 60F in a \nfew locations. Another chilly night is expected with ideal \nradiational cooling conditions once again with mostly clear \nskies/light to calm winds with lows falling into the mid 20s to \nlower 30s. Not much change with the sensible weather to close \nthe weekend with the H500 ridge over the lower MS River Valley \nfolding over into the Northeast, and the sfc high very slowly \ndrifting south and east of the forecast area heading into Sunday\nnight. Temps rise slightly above normal to close the weekend in\nthe 50s to lower 60s across the forecast area.\n\nThe mid and upper level flow becomes more zonal aloft to start the \nweek, as the H500 ridge is slightly surpressed to the south and \neast.  Some patchy valley fog may develop Mon morning with the sfc \nanticyclone south/southeast of the region. Low temps look more \nseasonable. A mositure-starved cold front approaches Mon with \nlimited low-level moisture. Some of the deterministic guidance \nand ensembles have some light showers impacting the western \nMohawk Valley and western Adirondacks late Mon, but have \nmaintained a dry forecast based on the NBM for now. The cold \nfront brings a reinforcing shot of cold advection, as a \nCanadian surface high builds in again for Tue-Wed with temps \nnear to slightly below normal and continued dry conditions. The \nnext best chance of showers may not occur until late Thu into \nFri with a northern stream systems warm and cold fronts \napproaching from the southwest/west based on some of the medium\nrange deterministic/ensemble/NBM guidance. Precipitation looks \nlight with this system.",
